---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import { withUtm, track } from '../../lib/links';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const listingSlug = 'access-governance-reporter';
const listing = withUtm('https://marketplace.atlassian.com/apps/3162504883/access-governance-reporter', listingSlug, 'blog-post');
const listingTrack = track('atlassian', listingSlug, 'blog-post');
---
<BlogPost
  title="Atlassian's own script for finding group usage in Jira, and what it misses"
  description="Atlassian's support KB hands you a Python script that finds a group's permission-scheme usage and then tells you it is unsupported and incomplete. Here is what the script covers, what it misses, and how to close the gap."
  tag="Jira administration"
  pubDate="September 2, 2026"
  readMins={6}
>
  <p>
    Ask any Jira admin who has tried to delete a group: before you remove it, you need to know every
    place it is used, or something breaks quietly three weeks later. The feature request for a native
    group-usage view has been open on the Jira issue tracker for years and has collected over a
    thousand votes. Atlassian's answer, for now, lives in a support KB article: a Python script you
    run yourself against the REST API. The same article tells you the script is beyond Atlassian
    Support's scope, and that permission schemes are not the only place groups are used. Both
    statements are worth taking seriously.
  </p>

  <h2>What the KB script actually does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Calls <code>GET /rest/api/3/permissionscheme?expand=permissions</code> to pull every
      permission scheme and its grants.</li>
    <li>Loops through the grants looking for your target group name.</li>
    <li>Prints the names of the schemes that reference the group.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That is a useful start, and for a one-off check on a small site it may be all you need. But the
    output is a list of scheme names, not answers. It does not tell you which projects use those
    schemes, which permissions the group actually holds there, or who is in the group and therefore
    inherits all of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where groups hide beyond permission schemes</h2>
  <p>
    The KB's own caveat is the important part. A group can also sit in any of these, and the script
    sees none of them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Project roles</strong>, per project, plus the default role actors applied to every
      new project.</li>
    <li><strong>Notification schemes</strong>, so deleting the group silently stops emails.</li>
    <li><strong>Issue security levels</strong>, so members lose visibility of restricted issues.</li>
    <li><strong>Filter sharing and edit grants</strong>, <code>membersOf()</code> clauses inside
      filter JQL, and filter subscriptions.</li>
    <li><strong>Dashboard sharing</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Product access</strong> (application roles), which is how the group may be granting
      Jira seats.</li>
    <li><strong>Group-picker custom fields</strong>, workflow conditions and validators, automation
      rules, and board permissions.</li>
    <li><strong>Global permissions</strong>, where Jira Cloud has no public API that returns the
      grantees at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Extending the DIY approach</h2>
  <p>
    If you want to stay in script land, the honest version of the job looks like this:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Start from the KB script's scheme scan, then resolve schemes to projects with
      <code>GET /rest/api/3/project/&#123;id&#125;/permissionscheme</code> so you know where each
      grant actually applies.</li>
    <li>Walk <code>GET /rest/api/3/project/&#123;id&#125;/role</code> and each role's actors to catch
      role-based grants, which the scheme scan misses entirely.</li>
    <li>Add per-surface passes for notification schemes, issue security schemes, filters, and
      dashboards, each with its own pagination and its own edge cases.</li>
    <li>Keep a completeness note per surface, because some (global permissions above all) cannot be
      enumerated via the public API, and an audit that silently omits them is worse than one that
      says so.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One admin on the feature-request thread reported his home-grown version of this makes roughly
    two thousand REST requests per run. It works, until the person who wrote it leaves, or an API
    changes, or the security review asks where the script runs and what token it uses.
  </p>

  <h2>A ready-made option: Access Governance Reporter</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Our tool for this:</strong> <a class="text-brand font-semibold" href={listing} {...listingTrack} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Access Governance Reporter</a>
    on the Atlassian Marketplace. It is a Forge app that runs entirely on Atlassian's platform inside
    your Jira Cloud tenant; no data leaves your instance.
  </div>
  <p>
    Access Governance Reporter is the productized version of the pattern above, in both directions.
    It crawls permission schemes with their grants, resolves them to the projects that use them, and
    walks every project role and its actors. From that it answers the group question the KB script
    starts on: for any group, every project and permission it grants, and whether the grant comes
    from a scheme or a role. It also answers the reverse question the script cannot: for any user,
    the full effective-access view, every project and permission with the exact group or role that
    grants it. A summary-first dashboard loads instantly at any site size, everything is searchable
    server-side, and one click exports audit-ready CSV for access reviews.
  </p>

  <h2>Key facts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The native gap:</strong> Jira Cloud has no built-in view of where a group is used;
      the thousand-vote feature request remains open.</li>
    <li><strong>Atlassian's DIY answer:</strong> an unsupported Python script covering permission
      schemes only, with the KB itself noting groups also live in global permissions, filter shares,
      dashboards, and more.</li>
    <li><strong>The durable fix:</strong> a crawl that resolves schemes to projects and includes
      role actors, with honest completeness notes for surfaces the API cannot enumerate.</li>
    <li><strong>Least privilege:</strong> the read path needs only read scopes; nothing in Jira is
      modified by reporting.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="mt-6">
    Related reading:
    <a class="text-brand" href={`${base}/blog/keep-atlassian-audit-logs-beyond-retention`}>Jira's audit log has the same DIY-script answer for retention: here is how to keep records past the window</a>.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
